+# Security / Release-Integrity Independent Review
+
+- Reviewer model: GPT-5.5
+- Review role: independent security/release-integrity reviewer
+- Checkpoint SHA reviewed: `f8c87ab15c398402b0c04b4f96e85d28df6237f7`
+- Worktree reviewed: `/Users/pchordia/Documents/wip_apps/platforms/priyanshchordia.com-wt/CURSOR-C4-36H-20260907`
+- Verdict: Request changes
+
+## Scope Reviewed
+
+- `scripts/privacy_adversarial_harness.py`
+- `scripts/release_receipt_hardening.py`
+- `scripts/validate_release_receipt.py`
+- `scripts/clean_checkout_verify.py`
+- `scripts/local_verify.py`
+- `scripts/code_tree_digest.py`
+- `site/waitlist.js`, `site/app-themes.js`, plus mirrored `scripts/` copies
+- Evidence under `session-c4-evidence/privacy` and `session-c4-evidence/receipt`
+
+No installs, deploys, pushes, commits, or external sends were performed.
+
+## Findings
+
+1. High - C4 clean-checkout proof and committed release evidence are incomplete
+
+   At the reviewed checkpoint, the committed C4 evidence contains privacy, browser/perf/SEO/static/visual summaries and receipt hardening output, but no committed C4 release receipt and no C4 clean-checkout validation artifact. During review, a later untracked `session-c4-evidence/release_receipt.json` appeared with `source_sha` / `git_head_at_check` set to `f8c87ab15c398402b0c04b4f96e85d28df6237f7`, but `session-c4-evidence/clean_checkout/clean_checkout_verify.json` and `session-c4-evidence/local_verify_summary.json` were still absent.
+
+   Why it matters: release-integrity approval should be based on durable evidence at the reviewed checkpoint. The current evidence chain does not show a complete C4 `local_verify.py` run through clean-checkout validation.
+
+   Recommendation: run the C4 release/local verification path so it emits and preserves `session-c4-evidence/release_receipt.json`, `session-c4-evidence/local_verify_summary.json`, and `session-c4-evidence/clean_checkout/clean_checkout_verify.json`, then validate the receipt with live code-tree recomputation. Commit or otherwise freeze those artifacts before presenting the checkpoint for approval.
+
+2. High - C4 receipt generation rewrites/depends on prior-session evidence paths
+
+   `scripts/release_check.py` hardcodes browser and interaction evidence output under `session-c2-evidence/...`. When `scripts/local_verify.py` invokes release_check for C4, the generated C4 receipt records successful browser/interaction steps whose `--evidence` paths point to `session-c2-evidence/browser` and `session-c2-evidence/interaction`. The worktree also showed modified C2 evidence files after this run.
+
+   Why it matters: C4 verification should not mutate prior-session evidence or rely on C2 paths for C4 proof. Cross-session evidence contamination weakens auditability and makes it harder to tell which evidence belongs to which checkpoint.
+
+   Recommendation: parameterize `release_check.py` evidence roots, and have `local_verify.py` pass C4-scoped browser/interaction evidence directories. Treat prior-session evidence as immutable once used as a baseline.
+
+3. High - Clean-checkout digest comparison can pass by construction
+
+   In `scripts/clean_checkout_verify.py`, the `archive_digest_matches_live_when_same_sha` case is considered passing when `digest["source"] == "filesystem-walk"`. A `git archive` extract has no `.git`, so its digest source is expected to be `filesystem-walk`; this makes the archive/live equality check pass even when the archive digest differs from the live worktree digest.
+
+   Why it matters: this weakens clean-checkout assurance and can mask dirty tracked product/code changes in the live worktree when comparing HEAD archive output to the current filesystem.
+
+   Recommendation: remove the filesystem-walk bypass from the equality case. If filesystem-vs-git enumeration differences are expected, normalize and assert the file lists/digests explicitly, or require `git diff --quiet`/`git diff --cached --quiet` for code-tree paths before treating archive/live equivalence as proven.
+
+4. Medium - C4 receipt hardening evidence uses a sample receipt with stale source SHA and dummy artifact hashes
+
+   `session-c4-evidence/receipt/sample_hardening_receipt.json` is explicitly a minimal sample, not a release receipt. It records `source_sha` / `git_head_at_check` as `43b48dd3e6da05b77475b1cc5cb6f0a8c501fcb6`, while the checkpoint under review is `f8c87ab15c398402b0c04b4f96e85d28df6237f7`, and its artifact hashes are placeholder `a*64`, `b*64`, and `c*64` values.
+
+   Why it matters: the hardening evidence is useful as a harness self-test, but it should not be treated as a release receipt or artifact integrity proof for Session C4.
+
+   Recommendation: label the sample evidence even more prominently in any index/return packet, and pair it with a real C4 release receipt before making release-integrity claims.
+
+5. Medium - Symlink containment check uses unsafe string-prefix containment
+
+   `scripts/release_receipt_hardening.py` checks symlink escape with `str(target).startswith(str(root))`. A sibling path whose prefix begins with the root string can pass this check even though it is outside the worktree.
+
+   Why it matters: symlink/path containment is a core release-integrity boundary. String-prefix checks are a known containment footgun.
+
+   Recommendation: use `target.relative_to(root)` inside a `try`/`except ValueError`, or use `os.path.commonpath([root, target]) == str(root)`. Add a regression where root is `/tmp/root` and target is `/tmp/root-evil/...`.
+
+6. Medium - Privacy evidence PII detector can miss real emails when fixture emails appear later in the same serialized payload
+
+   `PII_EMAIL_PATTERN` uses `(?!.*@fixture\.test)` ahead of the email match. In `assert_evidence_no_raw_pii`, the whole evidence object is serialized before scanning. If any `@fixture.test` value appears later in that JSON string, the negative lookahead can suppress detection of a real non-fixture email earlier in the payload. The same issue can affect `redact_evidence_value` for strings containing both real emails and a fixture later in the same string.
+
+   Why it matters: the privacy harness can falsely report `pii_leak_check: []` despite a non-fixture email in evidence.
+
+   Recommendation: match all email-like substrings first, then filter matches whose domain is exactly `fixture.test` or whose full value is in the allowed fixture set. Add mixed-payload tests with `real.user@example.com` before and after a fixture value.
+
+7. Medium - Receipt validation does not enforce artifact digest shape or artifact count consistency
+
+   `scripts/validate_release_receipt.py` recomputes the artifact tree hash from whatever string values are present, but it does not require each artifact digest to be 64 lowercase hex characters, and it does not require `artifact_count == len(artifact_hashes)`.
+
+   Why it matters: malformed or incomplete receipts can still be internally self-consistent if resealed, reducing audit quality and making tamper/path investigations harder.
+
+   Recommendation: fail closed on malformed digest values and artifact-count mismatches. Consider normalizing artifact keys before hashing and rejecting empty path segments or control characters.
+
+8. Low - Evidence includes local absolute paths and machine details
+
+   C4 evidence includes local paths such as `/Users/pchordia/...`, Chrome paths, ports, and platform details. I did not find real user-submitted waitlist emails in C4 privacy evidence; the only emails found were synthetic `@fixture.test` fixtures and the public support address.
+
+   Why it matters: this is acceptable for local session evidence, but it is not suitable for public release artifacts without redaction.
+
+   Recommendation: keep these files internal, or add a publication-safe redaction pass before sharing externally.
+
+## Positive Notes
+
+- `waitlist.js` prevents default submission, never claims success, avoids storing entered email in local/session storage, and writes status via `textContent` rather than HTML sinks.
+- `app-themes.js` constrains theme values to picker options, stores only theme preferences, catches storage failures, and writes theme descriptions via `textContent`.
+- Focused script search found no deploy/publish commands or non-loopback network sends in the reviewed Python harnesses.
+- `session-c4-evidence/privacy/privacy_adversarial_summary.json` reports static scans clean, 11 live adversarial cases passing, no harness errors, and `pii_leak_check: []`; note finding 5 above limits confidence in the PII check implementation.
+
+## Disposition Recommendations
+
+1. Block release-integrity approval until C4 release receipt, C4 local verification summary, and C4 clean-checkout verification artifacts exist durably for this checkpoint or for an explicitly documented equivalent code-tree digest.
+2. Parameterize `release_check.py` evidence roots so C4 verification does not write into `session-c2-evidence`.
+3. Patch the clean-checkout digest comparison so filesystem fallback does not turn mismatch detection into an unconditional pass.
+4. Patch symlink containment to use path-aware containment rather than string prefixes.
+5. Patch privacy evidence email scanning to filter fixture emails after matching, not by broad negative lookahead.
+6. Tighten receipt validation for artifact digest format, artifact count, and suspicious artifact keys.
+7. Keep current waitlist/theme client behavior; I found no XSS, hidden submit, email persistence, tracker, or accidental publish issue in those reviewed surfaces.
